From: Runeon Contributors <dev@runeon.com>
Subject: [PATCH] ntdll: Report non-canonical accesses as #GP under Rosetta, opt-in.

On x86 an access to a non-canonical address raises a general
protection fault, which Windows reports as an access violation reading
address -1 (ExceptionInformation[0] = 0, [1] = ~0). Rosetta delivers it
as a memory fault at the address itself, which a program that probes
such addresses to check the CPU can tell apart.

With the 0157 Rosetta CPU fidelity policy on, report it as Windows
does. Without the policy nothing changes.

---
diff --git a/dlls/ntdll/unix/signal_x86_64.c b/dlls/ntdll/unix/signal_x86_64.c
index dbf2bdb..dd72669 100644
--- a/dlls/ntdll/unix/signal_x86_64.c
+++ b/dlls/ntdll/unix/signal_x86_64.c
@@ -2726,6 +2726,22 @@ static void segv_handler( int signal, siginfo_t *siginfo, void *sigcontext )
         break;
     case TRAP_x86_PAGEFLT:  /* Page fault */
         rec.NumberParameters = 2;
+#ifdef __APPLE__
+        /* x86 raises #GP, not a page fault, for a non-canonical address, and
+         * Windows reports it as a read of address -1. Rosetta reports the address. */
+        if (rosetta_cpu_fidelity)
+        {
+            LONG64 addr = (LONG64)siginfo->si_addr;
+
+            if ((addr >> 47) != 0 && (addr >> 47) != -1)
+            {
+                rec.ExceptionCode = EXCEPTION_ACCESS_VIOLATION;
+                rec.ExceptionInformation[0] = 0;
+                rec.ExceptionInformation[1] = 0xffffffffffffffff;
+                break;
+            }
+        }
+#endif
         rec.ExceptionInformation[0] = (ERROR_sig(ucontext) >> 1) & 0x09;
         rec.ExceptionInformation[1] = (ULONG_PTR)siginfo->si_addr;
         if (!virtual_handle_fault( &rec, (void *)RSP_sig(ucontext) ) || check_invalid_gsbase( ucontext ))
